{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration MUST\nbe included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the subject meets the requirement. The one weakness is test marking, described below.\n\n**Implementation.** `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` (lines 42–173) is marked `{impl}` for `requirements.authoring_input`.\n- It always makes `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }` the first selector.\n- That selector is resolved by `source_input` and `to_model_input`, and its result is pushed into `mandatory` before any other input.\n- The resulting input carries the declaration's raw lines, including the opening `:::{req} name` fence and the closing `:::`. The mandatory input shown for this very subject has exactly that shape.\n- This happens whether the subject is a requirement or an example, because only terms are rejected. Both kinds go through the same path.\n- Only generated-evidence outline selectors are dropped. A `Requirements` selector can never be dropped, because `generated_evidence_selector` returns false for it. Mandatory inputs therefore always contain the subject.\n- The remaining code in the function is marked for the other requirements it implements: `mandatory_inputs`, `examples.authoring_inputs`, `references.authoring_inputs`, `definitions.authoring_inputs`, `read_denial` and so on. I found no unmarked behaviour that belongs to this requirement. The helpers `source_input` and `insert_selector` are marked for `mandatory_inputs`.\n\n**Tests marked `{test}` for this requirement.**\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` prepares both a requirement (`requirements.ready`) and an example (`requirements.case`). For the example it asserts that `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`, so the subject comes first. It also asserts that earlier evidence inputs are not carried forward.\n- `mandatory_git_inputs_require_staging_declarations_sources_and_terms` checks that the declaration file (`md/requirements.md`) must be readable before preparation succeeds.\n\n**Related tests not marked for this requirement.**\n- `unresolvable_earlier_inputs_do_not_block_reassessment` asserts the exact mandatory input `{\"requirements\": \"requirements.ready\", \"text\": [\":::{req} ready\", \"The project MUST be ready.\", \":::\"]}`. That is the complete fenced declaration.\n- `anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs` asserts the same exact first input.\n- `requirements_inputs_omit_option_lines` in `src/evidence.rs` checks `raw_lines` for the declaration.\n\nThe tests marked for this requirement are weaker than these. They check that the subject is present and first, but not the fence text. The unmarked tests do cover the exact full-declaration text, so the design space is covered: requirement, example, full fence text, and the subject's presence alongside other contributions. The marking is imperfect, but it is not a functional gap.",
